1
00:00:25,793 --> 00:00:27,993
♪ ♪

2
00:00:36,337 --> 00:00:39,737
♪ ♪

3
00:00:49,316 --> 00:00:52,217
♪ ♪

4
00:01:20,881 --> 00:01:23,114
♪ ♪

5
00:01:49,042 --> 00:01:51,242
♪ ♪

6
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:09,160
♪ ♪

7
00:02:30,383 --> 00:02:34,218
♪ Mairzy doats and dozy
doats and liddle lamzy divey ♪

8
00:02:34,287 --> 00:02:37,522
♪ A kiddley divey
too, wouldn't you? ♪

9
00:02:37,591 --> 00:02:39,624
Hi!

10
00:02:39,693 --> 00:02:42,171
Hi. ♪ Mairzy doats and dozy
doats and liddle lamzy divey... ♪

11
00:02:42,195 --> 00:02:43,762
Seen today's "Peach Pit"?

12
00:02:43,830 --> 00:02:45,675
What's that?

13
00:02:45,699 --> 00:02:47,398
The comic strip.

14
00:02:47,467 --> 00:02:49,579
I don't read the funnies.

15
00:02:49,603 --> 00:02:51,703
Who'd you vote for in
the state senate election?

16
00:02:51,772 --> 00:02:53,016
♪ Do do do do do. ♪

17
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:54,639
Peter Dolan.

18
00:02:54,708 --> 00:02:58,376
Then you should start
reading the funnies.

19
00:02:58,445 --> 00:03:00,379
Huh.

20
00:03:00,447 --> 00:03:02,280
That looks just like him.

21
00:03:02,349 --> 00:03:03,793
That's the whole idea.

22
00:03:03,817 --> 00:03:05,616
Who's he talking to?

23
00:03:05,685 --> 00:03:07,452
Some married woman.

24
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:09,888
Up till now, she's just
been a voice on the phone,

25
00:03:09,957 --> 00:03:12,257
but it looks like we're
finally going to see her.

26
00:03:12,326 --> 00:03:13,536
Huh.

27
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:16,929
It says the artist is going
to take a couple days off.

28
00:03:16,997 --> 00:03:18,663
Milking the suspense.

29
00:03:18,732 --> 00:03:21,799
Everybody in Georgia's
dying to know who she is.

30
00:03:21,868 --> 00:03:26,538
Oh, he wouldn't draw a person
that people would recognize.

31
00:03:26,606 --> 00:03:27,638
Sure, he would.

32
00:03:27,707 --> 00:03:29,852
Well, that would be libel.

33
00:03:29,876 --> 00:03:31,554
Defamation of character.

34
00:03:31,578 --> 00:03:33,211
Not if it's true.

35
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:35,614
Ron Winfield's been doing
this strip for seven years.

36
00:03:35,682 --> 00:03:37,115
He's never, ever lost a suit.

37
00:03:37,183 --> 00:03:38,327
Huh.

38
00:03:38,351 --> 00:03:40,385
It's a lie.

39
00:03:40,453 --> 00:03:42,453
So, we sue him.

40
00:03:42,522 --> 00:03:45,991
No. No, I don't want to sue him.

41
00:03:47,327 --> 00:03:49,928
I want to stop him.

42
00:03:49,997 --> 00:03:52,931
You know, if and
when he puts a face

43
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:54,966
on this mystery
woman he's invented,

44
00:03:55,035 --> 00:03:56,434
it's going to be too late.

45
00:03:56,503 --> 00:03:58,336
The damage will have
already been done.

46
00:03:58,405 --> 00:03:59,782
Well, until he
crosses that line,

47
00:03:59,806 --> 00:04:02,540
I'm afraid we have
no legal recourse.

48
00:04:02,609 --> 00:04:06,311
I can't believe the creep
has a right to do this.

49
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:08,624
Now, you're my lawyer,
Sam. Do something.

50
00:04:08,648 --> 00:04:10,015
Now, Pete, look, I guarantee it.

51
00:04:10,083 --> 00:04:11,883
The minute he makes
a claim in his strip

52
00:04:11,952 --> 00:04:13,251
which he cannot prove,

53
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:15,420
we'll hit him with a lawsuit
that'll make him sorry

54
00:04:15,488 --> 00:04:17,422
he ever learned to draw.

55
00:04:36,310 --> 00:04:38,343
You better get going, dear.

56
00:04:38,412 --> 00:04:39,878
I thought you had a meeting.

57
00:04:39,947 --> 00:04:41,613
Yes. Yes, I do.

58
00:04:41,682 --> 00:04:43,581
We have that fundraiser tonight.

59
00:04:43,651 --> 00:04:45,416
I'll try to get home early.

60
00:05:01,902 --> 00:05:03,001
I'll keep trying.

61
00:05:05,272 --> 00:05:07,717
Let me know as soon as
you get a hold of his lawyer.

62
00:05:07,741 --> 00:05:08,807
Yeah.

63
00:05:08,876 --> 00:05:10,342
Did you see it?

64
00:05:10,410 --> 00:05:12,376
Yes, I did.

65
00:05:12,446 --> 00:05:14,979
What are we going to do?

66
00:05:15,048 --> 00:05:18,182
Well, the thing we absolutely
cannot afford to do is panic.

67
00:05:18,251 --> 00:05:19,350
My whole career

68
00:05:19,420 --> 00:05:21,152
is about to go up in flames.

69
00:05:21,221 --> 00:05:23,266
Peter, he not only knew
the name of the hotel,

70
00:05:23,290 --> 00:05:24,410
he knew the room number.

71
00:05:24,458 --> 00:05:25,557
Well, maybe he's, uh...

72
00:05:25,626 --> 00:05:28,059
Maybe he's not
going to expose you.

73
00:05:28,128 --> 00:05:30,073
Maybe he's going
to keep you offstage

74
00:05:30,097 --> 00:05:31,262
the whole time, huh?

75
00:05:31,331 --> 00:05:33,042
Ron Winfield never
pulled a punch in his life,

76
00:05:33,066 --> 00:05:33,998
and you know it.

77
00:05:34,067 --> 00:05:35,199
Look, we-we don't have

78
00:05:35,268 --> 00:05:38,069
all the facts yet.
We've got to stay calm.

79
00:05:38,138 --> 00:05:39,604
How can I?

80
00:05:39,673 --> 00:05:41,451
I'm the one who's
cheating. I'm the one

81
00:05:41,475 --> 00:05:43,653
who's going to look like a
tramp in this thing, not you.

82
00:05:43,677 --> 00:05:46,611
Sandra, I love you,

83
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:49,881
and no matter what happens,

84
00:05:49,950 --> 00:05:51,695
I'm not going to
abandon you. I promise.

85
00:05:51,719 --> 00:05:54,319
I'm sorry.

86
00:05:54,387 --> 00:05:58,689
I-I'm just so angry
and I'm scared.

87
00:05:58,758 --> 00:06:04,096
Well, you let me handle
things, and try... try not to worry.

88
00:06:04,164 --> 00:06:05,964
Peter?

89
00:06:06,032 --> 00:06:07,498
Yeah.

90
00:06:07,567 --> 00:06:09,968
I love you.

91
00:06:35,461 --> 00:06:37,195
Ron Winfield's office.

92
00:06:37,263 --> 00:06:40,431
Why, I don't know,
Senator Dolan.

93
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:43,568
I just got in myself. I'll
check to see if he's here.

94
00:06:45,171 --> 00:06:46,337
No, sir, he's not.

95
00:06:46,406 --> 00:06:48,339
May I take a message?

96
00:06:51,745 --> 00:06:53,978
I'm not quite familiar
with that word, Senator.

97
00:06:54,047 --> 00:06:55,279
Would you mind spelling it?

98
00:06:55,348 --> 00:06:57,749
Yes, sir. I'll be
sure and tell him.

99
00:06:59,820 --> 00:07:01,697
He said that if you don't get
back to him within the hour,

100
00:07:01,721 --> 00:07:02,954
you'll be...

101
00:07:03,023 --> 00:07:04,622
roughly paraphrased,
in deep trouble.

102
00:07:04,691 --> 00:07:06,991
Okay.

103
00:07:09,129 --> 00:07:11,429
Ron Winfield's office.

104
00:07:11,498 --> 00:07:13,343
Uh, I don't know,
Senator Hopkins.

105
00:07:13,367 --> 00:07:15,345
I just got in myself. I'll
check to see if he's in.

106
00:07:15,369 --> 00:07:16,968
Do you have anything to say

107
00:07:17,036 --> 00:07:19,670
to the husband of
Senator Dolan's lover?

108
00:07:21,274 --> 00:07:22,774
I'm sorry, sir.

109
00:07:22,843 --> 00:07:25,277
He's not in. May I
take a messa... ge?

110
00:07:33,219 --> 00:07:35,953
God, I'm good.

111
00:07:36,022 --> 00:07:40,825
Actually, I'd make
the lines a little bolder.

112
00:07:40,894 --> 00:07:43,461
You know, there's a moment
when all my assistants decide

113
00:07:43,530 --> 00:07:44,707
they're just as
talented as I am.

114
00:07:44,731 --> 00:07:46,331
Is that what we're
talking about here?

115
00:07:46,400 --> 00:07:49,301
I think I'm a better draftsman.

116
00:07:49,369 --> 00:07:52,103
You are, but don't
let it go to your head.

117
00:07:52,172 --> 00:07:55,706
It's not enough. What you
need is an instinct for the jugular,

118
00:07:55,775 --> 00:07:59,177
dedication to
concept, the message.

119
00:07:59,246 --> 00:08:00,578
I'm gonna miss my plane.

120
00:08:05,085 --> 00:08:06,462
So, who you going after now?

121
00:08:06,486 --> 00:08:07,719
You'll see.

122
00:08:07,787 --> 00:08:09,165
Ink the prints. That's
all you want me to do?

123
00:08:09,189 --> 00:08:10,855
Yeah, right. And, uh, uh...

124
00:08:10,924 --> 00:08:12,202
Feed Otis. Feed Otis.

125
00:08:12,226 --> 00:08:13,369
I'll call you when I get back.

126
00:08:13,393 --> 00:08:15,327
Bye.

127
00:08:47,761 --> 00:08:50,528
What time did he call?

128
00:08:50,597 --> 00:08:53,498
Oh.

129
00:08:53,566 --> 00:08:55,800
All right, all right. What else?

130
00:08:55,868 --> 00:08:58,636
I don't need that.

131
00:08:58,705 --> 00:09:00,405
All right. That's it?

132
00:09:00,473 --> 00:09:02,873
Okay. All right. Thanks!

133
00:09:21,995 --> 00:09:23,628
Are you Ron Winfield?

134
00:09:23,697 --> 00:09:25,430
Well, hello, Senator.

135
00:09:25,498 --> 00:09:27,065
All by yourself tonight?

136
00:09:27,133 --> 00:09:29,067
Come on in.

137
00:09:34,308 --> 00:09:36,552
A little early to be canvassing
the neighborhood for votes,

138
00:09:36,576 --> 00:09:38,242
isn't it, Senator?

139
00:09:39,879 --> 00:09:41,045
You know why I'm here.

140
00:09:42,215 --> 00:09:44,615
No.

141
00:09:44,685 --> 00:09:46,584
It's too heavy.

142
00:09:46,653 --> 00:09:48,053
I don't like this. Get him down.

143
00:09:48,121 --> 00:09:49,220
Get him off me.

144
00:09:49,288 --> 00:09:51,122
Otis, over there and lie down.

145
00:09:56,797 --> 00:09:58,374
Does that to everyone.
Don't feel flattered.

146
00:09:58,398 --> 00:10:01,032
I don't.

147
00:10:01,100 --> 00:10:05,169
Let's talk about your next
comic strip, Mr. Winfield.

148
00:10:05,238 --> 00:10:09,707
Okay. Let's.

149
00:10:09,776 --> 00:10:11,987
When you two are alone,
do you call Miss Hopkins

150
00:10:12,011 --> 00:10:13,122
Sandra or Sandy?

151
00:10:13,146 --> 00:10:14,745
I'm a stickler for details.

152
00:10:14,814 --> 00:10:16,580
You can't do this.

153
00:10:16,650 --> 00:10:18,582
Already have.

154
00:10:21,988 --> 00:10:24,066
I think I got the hairdo
down pretty good,

155
00:10:24,090 --> 00:10:25,370
don't you think?

156
00:10:30,597 --> 00:10:33,264
You're going to ruin both
of us if you publish that.

157
00:10:33,333 --> 00:10:35,433
You are an elected
official and so is she.

158
00:10:35,502 --> 00:10:37,179
The public has a right
to know these things.

159
00:10:37,203 --> 00:10:39,336
Why? We're not criminals.

160
00:10:39,405 --> 00:10:41,172
Why?

161
00:10:41,241 --> 00:10:43,674
She's a married woman.

162
00:10:43,743 --> 00:10:46,377
It's people like you who are
destroying the moral fabric

163
00:10:46,446 --> 00:10:48,012
of this great nation.

164
00:10:48,081 --> 00:10:51,816
Driving that most precious
of American institutions...

165
00:10:51,884 --> 00:10:54,152
The family... to the
brink of extinction.

166
00:10:54,221 --> 00:10:56,621
Oh, please spare me.

167
00:10:56,689 --> 00:11:00,591
However, the fact
of the matter is,

168
00:11:00,660 --> 00:11:02,871
no one outside myself and
my assistant has seen this,

169
00:11:02,895 --> 00:11:04,407
so maybe we can make a deal.

170
00:11:04,431 --> 00:11:05,575
You want to make a deal?

171
00:11:05,599 --> 00:11:07,431
Blackmail?

172
00:11:08,902 --> 00:11:11,047
You piece of trash. Hey,

173
00:11:11,071 --> 00:11:12,481
Senator, let's not
start throwing stones.

174
00:11:12,505 --> 00:11:14,583
You're the one who's got
this thing about sleeping

175
00:11:14,607 --> 00:11:16,853
with his colleague's wife.
How much do you want?

176
00:11:16,877 --> 00:11:18,376
Ooh.

177
00:11:18,445 --> 00:11:20,512
Money's not everything.

178
00:11:25,051 --> 00:11:27,497
You don't want
me to publish this...

179
00:11:27,521 --> 00:11:30,454
give me something bigger
and better to replace it.

180
00:11:30,523 --> 00:11:32,557
I see.

181
00:11:32,625 --> 00:11:35,659
Trade<i> my</i> dirt for somebody
else's dirt, is that it?

182
00:11:35,728 --> 00:11:37,394
Yeah.

183
00:11:37,463 --> 00:11:39,230
Forget it.

184
00:11:39,299 --> 00:11:42,834
Well, then, Senator,
I guess I'll see you...

185
00:11:42,902 --> 00:11:45,002
in the funny papers.

186
00:11:46,740 --> 00:11:47,938
Hey!

187
00:11:58,118 --> 00:11:59,917
Ow!

188
00:12:18,437 --> 00:12:20,149
Yeah, sure, go ahead, take it!

189
00:12:20,173 --> 00:12:22,218
I'll just draw another one,

190
00:12:22,242 --> 00:12:24,708
Einstein!

191
00:12:29,216 --> 00:12:31,282
Oh, yeah, uh...

192
00:12:31,351 --> 00:12:32,984
yeah, okay, thanks, Otis.

193
00:12:53,272 --> 00:12:55,006
Come on, Otis.

194
00:13:03,216 --> 00:13:04,816
What do you mean,

195
00:13:04,884 --> 00:13:06,483
you<i> think</i> you killed him?

196
00:13:07,887 --> 00:13:10,321
All I know is that...

197
00:13:10,390 --> 00:13:12,089
he hit me, I hit him,

198
00:13:12,158 --> 00:13:14,525
and he fell back
and hit his head,

199
00:13:14,593 --> 00:13:16,327
hard, on the fireplace.

200
00:13:16,396 --> 00:13:18,663
Did you knock him
out? No. He, uh,

201
00:13:18,732 --> 00:13:21,633
he was dazed, but he
was never unconscious.

202
00:13:21,701 --> 00:13:24,569
At least... not before I left.

203
00:13:24,638 --> 00:13:26,137
Well, what were you doing there?

204
00:13:26,205 --> 00:13:29,340
I came to beg him not to run

205
00:13:29,408 --> 00:13:31,842
the next installment
of his comic strip.

206
00:13:31,912 --> 00:13:35,546
Oh, yeah, the one
where he was gonna say

207
00:13:35,615 --> 00:13:38,282
who the woman is
that you're seeing.

208
00:13:38,351 --> 00:13:40,585
He even tried to
blackmail me, but then...

209
00:13:40,654 --> 00:13:43,888
that's when I got
mad, grabbed the strip

210
00:13:43,956 --> 00:13:45,690
and took off.

211
00:13:45,758 --> 00:13:47,436
After you punched him out.

212
00:13:47,460 --> 00:13:48,893
Mm-hmm.

213
00:13:50,430 --> 00:13:53,431
Looks like Sandra Hopkins.

214
00:13:55,001 --> 00:13:57,402
That's who it is.

215
00:14:02,008 --> 00:14:05,848
The State Superintendent
of Schools?

216
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:08,279
Yes.

217
00:14:08,348 --> 00:14:11,416
Senator David Hopkins' wife?

218
00:14:11,484 --> 00:14:12,783
Yes.

219
00:14:12,852 --> 00:14:15,398
That's who you've been
having an affair with?

220
00:14:15,422 --> 00:14:16,654
Yes.

221
00:14:16,723 --> 00:14:20,291
It wasn't something I set
out to do; it just happened.

222
00:14:20,359 --> 00:14:22,993
How did this Winfield
fellow find out?

223
00:14:23,062 --> 00:14:24,495
I have no idea.

224
00:14:24,564 --> 00:14:26,975
Huh. But he was
alive when you left?

225
00:14:26,999 --> 00:14:29,044
Mm-hmm. He must
have died later on from...

226
00:14:29,068 --> 00:14:30,467
from a blood clot or something,

227
00:14:30,537 --> 00:14:32,348
that's the only
thing I can figure.

228
00:14:35,608 --> 00:14:39,377
Well... best thing for you to
do is go down and tell the police

229
00:14:39,445 --> 00:14:41,012
what you just told me.

230
00:14:41,080 --> 00:14:43,046
I had a feeling that was what

231
00:14:43,115 --> 00:14:44,214
you were gonna say.

232
00:14:44,283 --> 00:14:46,462
It won't be pleasant,
and your career

233
00:14:46,486 --> 00:14:48,519
probably won't be
on an upswing, but...

234
00:14:48,587 --> 00:14:50,587
they have to know

235
00:14:50,656 --> 00:14:52,434
that if you killed
him on purpose,

236
00:14:52,458 --> 00:14:54,436
you'd be halfway
around the world by now.

237
00:14:54,460 --> 00:14:56,160
You don't think they're
gonna charge me

238
00:14:56,229 --> 00:14:58,306
with murder? No.
Involuntary manslaughter,

239
00:14:58,330 --> 00:15:00,698
maybe, but not-not murder.

240
00:15:00,766 --> 00:15:02,667
D.A.'d never get

241
00:15:02,735 --> 00:15:07,538
a conviction... they'd be
crazy to charge you with that.

242
00:15:07,607 --> 00:15:09,785
<i>First-degree murder?
You're out of your mind!</i>

243
00:15:09,809 --> 00:15:13,110
We have a very strong case.

244
00:15:13,179 --> 00:15:15,980
You're gonna have
to convince a jury

245
00:15:16,049 --> 00:15:18,615
that Ronald Winfield's
death was premeditated.

246
00:15:18,684 --> 00:15:20,117
I know.

247
00:15:20,186 --> 00:15:21,618
But it was an accident.

248
00:15:21,687 --> 00:15:23,120
We can prove otherwise.

249
00:15:23,189 --> 00:15:26,290
But, Julie... We have a witness
who heard Senator Dolan

250
00:15:26,359 --> 00:15:28,292
threaten Winfield over
the phone that day.

251
00:15:28,361 --> 00:15:30,862
We have, uh, Senator
Dolan's statement,

252
00:15:30,930 --> 00:15:33,864
in which he admits getting
into a violent argument

253
00:15:33,933 --> 00:15:36,567
with Winfield that night,
and we have the comic strip,

254
00:15:36,635 --> 00:15:39,971
the one Senator Dolan stole
in hopes of keeping his affair

255
00:15:40,039 --> 00:15:43,318
with Sandra Hopkins a secret.
Yeah, because he gave it to you.

256
00:15:43,342 --> 00:15:46,043
He just wanted to make
himself look innocent.

257
00:15:49,582 --> 00:15:51,748
You mad?

258
00:15:51,817 --> 00:15:54,985
You ruined my hot dog.

259
00:16:05,831 --> 00:16:08,799
Things started getting physical
over here around the desk.

260
00:16:08,868 --> 00:16:11,469
One of us knocked
that bottle of ink down.

261
00:16:11,537 --> 00:16:14,104
Mm.

262
00:16:14,173 --> 00:16:16,106
He hit his head here?

263
00:16:16,175 --> 00:16:19,276
Oh, no, actually, over here
on this side of the fireplace.

264
00:16:19,345 --> 00:16:21,278
And he fell right
around in here.

265
00:16:21,347 --> 00:16:23,159
Yeah? Well, the forensics people

266
00:16:23,183 --> 00:16:26,517
found traces of blood
and hair in two places...

267
00:16:26,586 --> 00:16:28,052
Here and over there.

268
00:16:28,121 --> 00:16:29,998
How many times did you hit him?

269
00:16:30,022 --> 00:16:31,022
Once.

270
00:16:31,057 --> 00:16:32,334
And in the autopsy report,

271
00:16:32,358 --> 00:16:34,870
it said he was hit
in the head twice.

272
00:16:34,894 --> 00:16:38,396
Ben, I only hit
him one time. Huh.

273
00:16:39,899 --> 00:16:41,733
Oh, oh, oh!

274
00:16:41,801 --> 00:16:44,379
What a good dog! Otis,
get off him. Lie down.

275
00:16:44,403 --> 00:16:47,149
You want to get down? You want...
you want to just get down now?

276
00:16:47,173 --> 00:16:49,773
Yeah, yeah.

277
00:16:53,246 --> 00:16:54,845
Oh, I'm...

278
00:16:54,914 --> 00:16:57,159
I'm Ben Matlock,
Senator Dolan's lawyer.

279
00:16:57,183 --> 00:17:00,151
Mickey Alder, Ron
Winfield's assistant. Hello.

280
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:02,364
Would you mind returning
those strips you stole last night?

281
00:17:02,388 --> 00:17:04,855
Uh, I only took one strip.

282
00:17:04,924 --> 00:17:07,269
You mean more
than one is missing?

283
00:17:07,293 --> 00:17:09,237
You bet. Three weeks' worth.

284
00:17:09,261 --> 00:17:12,340
How do you know? I saw them
sitting on his desk yesterday morning.

285
00:17:12,364 --> 00:17:14,999
When I came by last night
at 9:00, they were gone.

286
00:17:15,067 --> 00:17:16,267
And Ron was dead.

287
00:17:16,335 --> 00:17:18,368
Oh. Uh... Thanks.

288
00:17:18,437 --> 00:17:21,172
Senator, you want to just
run on back to the office,

289
00:17:21,241 --> 00:17:23,507
and I'll meet you
in a little bit. Fine.

290
00:17:23,576 --> 00:17:26,510
So, uh...

291
00:17:26,579 --> 00:17:30,280
you were
Mr. Winfield's assistant?

292
00:17:30,349 --> 00:17:32,817
That's what I said.

293
00:17:32,885 --> 00:17:34,384
You live here?

294
00:17:34,453 --> 00:17:36,721
Your employees live
with you, Mr. Matlock?

295
00:17:36,789 --> 00:17:38,266
No.

296
00:17:38,290 --> 00:17:41,470
I just wondered what you
were doing here at 9:00 at night.

297
00:17:41,494 --> 00:17:43,928
I told you, I was
taking care of the dog.

298
00:17:43,997 --> 00:17:45,574
Ron flew to Miami
yesterday for business,

299
00:17:45,598 --> 00:17:48,543
so I called the house around
8:00, the service picked up,

300
00:17:48,567 --> 00:17:50,846
and I came over
to feed Otis. Oh.

301
00:17:50,870 --> 00:17:53,004
Well, do you know if, uh,

302
00:17:53,072 --> 00:17:55,406
Mr. Winfield called
anybody else yesterday?

303
00:17:55,474 --> 00:17:57,174
Or did anybody call him?

304
00:17:57,243 --> 00:17:59,087
Well, yeah, I know
Senator Dolan called.

305
00:17:59,111 --> 00:18:01,845
He was really mad.
Mm. Anybody else?

306
00:18:01,914 --> 00:18:03,247
Yeah, Senator Hopkins.

307
00:18:03,315 --> 00:18:04,748
What did he want?

308
00:18:04,817 --> 00:18:07,062
Your guess is as good as mine.

309
00:18:07,086 --> 00:18:08,497
Hey, could you do me a favor?

310
00:18:08,521 --> 00:18:10,165
I really need to get
those strips back.

311
00:18:10,189 --> 00:18:11,666
Could you just talk
to Senator Dolan?

312
00:18:11,690 --> 00:18:12,923
He doesn't have them.

313
00:18:12,992 --> 00:18:14,257
Right.

314
00:18:16,962 --> 00:18:18,562
Uh...

315
00:18:20,065 --> 00:18:22,010
What do you want with them?

316
00:18:22,034 --> 00:18:24,034
Well, the people who
syndicate the strip

317
00:18:24,103 --> 00:18:25,403
are interested in taking some

318
00:18:25,471 --> 00:18:26,715
of the already
established characters

319
00:18:26,739 --> 00:18:28,038
and starting a new strip.

320
00:18:28,107 --> 00:18:30,240
You know, since Ron is...

321
00:18:30,309 --> 00:18:31,753
is dead. Mm-hmm.

322
00:18:31,777 --> 00:18:33,810
Who'd draw it?

323
00:18:34,914 --> 00:18:36,447
Well, probably me.

324
00:18:36,515 --> 00:18:38,215
You?

325
00:18:38,284 --> 00:18:39,817
Well, I am a cartoonist.

326
00:18:39,885 --> 00:18:42,419
I mean, I was just working
for Ron for the experience.

327
00:18:44,256 --> 00:18:45,489
You'll have to excuse me.

328
00:18:45,557 --> 00:18:47,158
I've got a lot of things to do.

329
00:18:47,226 --> 00:18:49,126
Got to find Otis a new home.

330
00:18:50,629 --> 00:18:52,196
Do you like dogs?

331
00:18:57,470 --> 00:18:59,169
Not... not that one.

332
00:19:14,186 --> 00:19:15,653
What's her name?

333
00:19:15,721 --> 00:19:17,120
Mickey Alder.

334
00:19:17,189 --> 00:19:20,123
She's going to inherit
Ron Winfield's comic strip?

335
00:19:20,192 --> 00:19:22,092
More or less.

336
00:19:22,161 --> 00:19:24,795
Isn't that a sweet deal?

337
00:19:24,863 --> 00:19:27,075
You think she could
have killed him?

338
00:19:27,099 --> 00:19:28,599
Well, if he was

339
00:19:28,668 --> 00:19:31,335
as woozy as you say
he was when you left,

340
00:19:31,404 --> 00:19:34,405
anybody could have come
in there and shoved his head

341
00:19:34,473 --> 00:19:37,741
in that fireplace one more
time... man or woman.

342
00:19:37,810 --> 00:19:41,278
Maybe I better have a
talk with Sandra Hopkins.

343
00:19:41,347 --> 00:19:44,460
Let's leave her
out of this, please.

344
00:19:44,484 --> 00:19:48,486
Her husband called Ron Winfield

345
00:19:48,554 --> 00:19:51,355
that morning just
after you did. Hmm.

346
00:19:51,424 --> 00:19:54,584
That must mean he knew about Sandra and me.
- Well...

347
00:19:57,229 --> 00:19:58,963
No.

348
00:20:23,122 --> 00:20:25,089
Uh, Mrs. Hopkins?

349
00:20:25,157 --> 00:20:26,590
I'm Michelle Thomas.

350
00:20:26,659 --> 00:20:29,727
I'm one of the attorneys
representing Senator Dolan.

351
00:20:29,796 --> 00:20:31,228
Do you have a minute?

352
00:20:32,331 --> 00:20:34,130
Uh, yes, uh...

353
00:20:34,199 --> 00:20:35,666
I'll meet you outside.

354
00:20:43,609 --> 00:20:46,477
I know about you and
the senator, Mrs. Hopkins.

355
00:20:47,580 --> 00:20:49,458
I guess it's just
a matter of time

356
00:20:49,482 --> 00:20:51,260
before the whole
world knows, isn't it?

357
00:20:51,284 --> 00:20:53,984
Is he all right?

358
00:20:54,053 --> 00:20:55,252
He misses you.

359
00:20:55,321 --> 00:20:56,987
He told me to tell you that.

360
00:20:58,091 --> 00:21:00,824
I've been afraid to call him.

361
00:21:00,893 --> 00:21:04,261
Does your husband know you
and Peter were having an affair?

362
00:21:04,329 --> 00:21:06,697
I don't think so.

363
00:21:06,766 --> 00:21:08,499
He's never said anything to me.

364
00:21:08,568 --> 00:21:11,435
How do you suppose
Ron Winfield found out?

365
00:21:11,504 --> 00:21:13,437
I have no idea.

366
00:21:13,506 --> 00:21:17,375
His cartoon would have hurt
you more than anyone else.

367
00:21:17,443 --> 00:21:19,510
Ms. Thomas, the
night of the murder,

368
00:21:19,579 --> 00:21:21,979
my husband and I
were at a fundraiser.

369
00:21:22,047 --> 00:21:23,346
You can verify this?

370
00:21:23,415 --> 00:21:24,815
Yes.

371
00:21:24,884 --> 00:21:26,361
We were there along
with several thousand

372
00:21:26,385 --> 00:21:28,230
of the governor's
closest friends.

373
00:21:28,254 --> 00:21:31,488
Then, I guess you
didn't kill Mr. Winfield.

374
00:21:32,658 --> 00:21:35,225
No... much as I'd have liked to.

375
00:21:36,996 --> 00:21:38,262
Thanks.

376
00:21:38,331 --> 00:21:40,797
I-I won't keep you any longer.

377
00:21:41,901 --> 00:21:44,802
Uh, tell Peter to...

378
00:21:44,870 --> 00:21:46,804
try not to worry.

379
00:22:19,438 --> 00:22:23,240
Oh, Sandra.

380
00:22:23,309 --> 00:22:25,508
I had to see you.

381
00:22:27,780 --> 00:22:30,514
Have I missed you.

382
00:22:30,582 --> 00:22:32,482
Peter...

383
00:22:32,551 --> 00:22:34,251
Wait, um...

384
00:22:35,654 --> 00:22:37,534
There's something I have to say.

385
00:22:42,461 --> 00:22:44,862
It's over.

386
00:22:44,931 --> 00:22:46,697
We're over.

387
00:22:46,766 --> 00:22:49,766
We should have known better.

388
00:22:49,835 --> 00:22:51,535
We did.

389
00:22:51,604 --> 00:22:54,638
Then we should have done better.

390
00:22:54,706 --> 00:22:56,806
I should have done better.

391
00:22:56,875 --> 00:22:58,808
This is my fault.

392
00:23:02,881 --> 00:23:05,482
I see.

393
00:23:05,551 --> 00:23:08,152
You forced me to
fall in love with you.

394
00:23:08,220 --> 00:23:09,853
Hmm?

395
00:23:24,637 --> 00:23:27,871
Oh, Peter, I'm a coward.

396
00:23:27,940 --> 00:23:32,710
I didn't have the courage
to end a loveless marriage,

397
00:23:32,778 --> 00:23:35,612
or to refuse the
love you offered me.

398
00:23:35,681 --> 00:23:38,749
I thought only of
myself, my career,

399
00:23:38,818 --> 00:23:41,985
how lonely I was
before I met you.

400
00:23:42,054 --> 00:23:45,188
I wanted to hang
on to everything.

401
00:23:47,059 --> 00:23:49,192
Now I've lost everything.

402
00:23:51,831 --> 00:23:53,731
You've still got me.

403
00:23:58,303 --> 00:23:59,448
I never meant...

404
00:23:59,472 --> 00:24:02,439
I know...

405
00:24:02,508 --> 00:24:04,908
To hurt me.

406
00:24:09,948 --> 00:24:15,051
Oh, what is going
to happen to us?

407
00:24:23,462 --> 00:24:26,730
Did you call Ron Winfield
the day he was murdered?

408
00:24:26,799 --> 00:24:28,944
Yes, I called Ron
Winfield that morning.

409
00:24:28,968 --> 00:24:30,567
About what?

410
00:24:30,636 --> 00:24:34,170
About his next strip, of course.

411
00:24:34,239 --> 00:24:38,141
The one where he
was going to reveal

412
00:24:38,210 --> 00:24:41,645
who Senator Dolan
was having an affair with?

413
00:24:41,714 --> 00:24:44,882
We both know it was
my wife, Mr. Matlock.

414
00:24:44,950 --> 00:24:47,551
How long had you known?

415
00:24:47,620 --> 00:24:48,886
About a month.

416
00:24:48,954 --> 00:24:50,532
I suspected she
was seeing someone,

417
00:24:50,556 --> 00:24:53,123
so I hired a
detective to follow her.

418
00:24:53,192 --> 00:24:54,892
Why didn't you confront her?

419
00:24:54,960 --> 00:24:59,563
Mr. Matlock, I've been in
the state senate ten years.

420
00:24:59,632 --> 00:25:01,343
I'm chairman of the
Public Works Committee

421
00:25:01,367 --> 00:25:03,111
and thinking of
running for congress.

422
00:25:03,135 --> 00:25:05,169
I don't need a divorce.

423
00:25:05,238 --> 00:25:08,071
Well, why did you
call Ron Winfield?

424
00:25:08,140 --> 00:25:10,552
I called him because if he
revealed Sandra's indiscretions

425
00:25:10,576 --> 00:25:12,788
the only thing I could
run for is dog catcher.

426
00:25:12,812 --> 00:25:17,214
Oh, you were going to try
to get him to kill the strip.

427
00:25:17,283 --> 00:25:18,982
Damn right.

428
00:25:19,051 --> 00:25:20,462
Only he never returned my call.

429
00:25:20,486 --> 00:25:23,587
Somebody took
care of it for you.

430
00:25:23,656 --> 00:25:27,591
All his murder did for me
was postpone the inevitable.

431
00:25:27,660 --> 00:25:30,694
Or are you going
to try to tell me

432
00:25:30,762 --> 00:25:32,807
my wife's affair won't be
made public during the trial?

433
00:25:32,831 --> 00:25:35,199
Oh, no, no, the cat'll
come out of the bag.

434
00:25:35,267 --> 00:25:37,033
Oh, played golf with
the vice president.

435
00:25:37,102 --> 00:25:38,168
Who won?

436
00:25:38,236 --> 00:25:40,370
I don't remember.

437
00:25:40,439 --> 00:25:42,572
Remember where you were
when Ron Winfield was killed?

438
00:25:44,276 --> 00:25:47,610
My wife and I were at a
very well-attended fundraiser.

439
00:25:47,680 --> 00:25:49,746
From 6:30 to almost midnight.

440
00:25:51,884 --> 00:25:55,151
You know, it's real easy to
slip out of those big parties

441
00:25:55,220 --> 00:25:57,220
unnoticed for a while.

442
00:25:57,289 --> 00:25:59,522
I do it all the time
when I'm bored.

443
00:25:59,591 --> 00:26:03,527
Well, I'll let you
get back to work.

444
00:26:03,596 --> 00:26:05,028
Thanks, Senator.

445
00:26:26,785 --> 00:26:28,051
Come on, boy.

446
00:26:36,995 --> 00:26:38,262
Hello, Otis.

447
00:26:38,330 --> 00:26:40,141
Hello. Otis, get down off him!

448
00:26:40,165 --> 00:26:41,932
Get over here.

449
00:26:47,974 --> 00:26:51,208
I haven't been able to find
anyone to take him yet. Oh.

450
00:26:51,277 --> 00:26:54,055
Well... What can I do for you?

451
00:26:54,079 --> 00:26:57,948
How about letting me in? Okay.

452
00:26:58,017 --> 00:27:00,583
Uh, those strips
that were stolen,

453
00:27:00,652 --> 00:27:03,687
did you ever get a look at 'em?

454
00:27:03,755 --> 00:27:06,023
I got a glance at 'em once.

455
00:27:06,092 --> 00:27:09,359
Well, do you have any
idea who or what your boss

456
00:27:09,428 --> 00:27:11,095
meant to expose next?

457
00:27:11,163 --> 00:27:13,330
No.

458
00:27:13,398 --> 00:27:16,800
Did he keep a diary
or a journal of any kind?

459
00:27:16,869 --> 00:27:18,335
Not that I know of.

460
00:27:18,403 --> 00:27:19,736
We can take a look.

461
00:27:29,381 --> 00:27:30,547
Hey, fella!

462
00:27:38,624 --> 00:27:40,457
Otis, get him. Get him.

463
00:27:40,526 --> 00:27:42,759
Get him, Otis. Get him.

464
00:27:42,827 --> 00:27:44,027
Otis, get him.

465
00:27:55,741 --> 00:27:57,941
Damn.

466
00:27:58,009 --> 00:28:01,878
Well, whatever
he was looking for,

467
00:28:01,947 --> 00:28:04,047
I guess he found it.

468
00:28:04,115 --> 00:28:06,717
What was special
about the word "sunset"?

469
00:28:06,785 --> 00:28:08,051
Sunset?

470
00:28:08,119 --> 00:28:10,019
Yeah, he used it in
almost every strip.

471
00:28:10,088 --> 00:28:11,688
Here, look here,
on the bus bench.

472
00:28:11,757 --> 00:28:12,789
See there?

473
00:28:12,858 --> 00:28:14,558
Oh, yeah.

474
00:28:14,626 --> 00:28:17,761
And here on... I
believe it's the book.

475
00:28:17,830 --> 00:28:18,695
Yeah, see?

476
00:28:18,763 --> 00:28:20,030
Sunset. He never said.

477
00:28:20,099 --> 00:28:21,698
He sure used it a lot.

478
00:28:21,767 --> 00:28:24,567
Ron did stuff like
that all the time.

479
00:28:24,636 --> 00:28:25,769
Here.

480
00:28:25,838 --> 00:28:27,537
Oh, yeah.

481
00:28:31,543 --> 00:28:35,045
Sunset Cleaners, Sunset Camera,
Sunset Flowers, Sunset Mortuary.

482
00:28:35,114 --> 00:28:37,247
There are more than 60
businesses in Atlanta alone

483
00:28:37,316 --> 00:28:38,915
with the word
"sunset" in the name.

484
00:28:38,984 --> 00:28:40,750
And there's a Sunset
Road, a Sunset Place

485
00:28:40,819 --> 00:28:41,851
and a Sunset Avenue.

486
00:28:41,920 --> 00:28:43,198
The man used the word "sunset"

487
00:28:43,222 --> 00:28:47,657
in his strip ten times
in the last six weeks.

488
00:28:47,726 --> 00:28:48,992
That's got to mean something.

489
00:28:49,061 --> 00:28:50,527
It might.

490
00:28:50,596 --> 00:28:53,029
As Dick Tracy used
to say, "I'll check it out."

491
00:28:58,837 --> 00:29:01,405
I want to testify
on my own behalf.

492
00:29:02,841 --> 00:29:04,274
No, you don't.

493
00:29:04,343 --> 00:29:06,009
Yes, I do.

494
00:29:06,078 --> 00:29:07,978
No, you don't.
Ben, I'm innocent.

495
00:29:08,046 --> 00:29:09,925
I want to get up
there and tell the truth.

496
00:29:09,949 --> 00:29:11,281
No, you don't.

497
00:29:11,350 --> 00:29:13,984
You'd be opening yourself
up for cross-examination.

498
00:29:14,052 --> 00:29:15,485
The prosecutor would bury you.

499
00:29:15,554 --> 00:29:18,188
I'd have no control
over her questions

500
00:29:18,256 --> 00:29:19,122
or your answers.

501
00:29:19,191 --> 00:29:20,890
I know this lady.

502
00:29:20,959 --> 00:29:22,392
She didn't vote for you.

503
00:29:22,461 --> 00:29:23,860
She believes you're guilty.

504
00:29:23,929 --> 00:29:26,129
I'm an elected official.

505
00:29:26,198 --> 00:29:29,599
Now, I get calls from
angry constituents

506
00:29:29,668 --> 00:29:33,003
and hostile reporters
every single day.

507
00:29:33,071 --> 00:29:34,871
I know how to field questions.

508
00:29:34,940 --> 00:29:35,805
No, you don't.

509
00:29:35,874 --> 00:29:38,008
This is different.

510
00:29:38,076 --> 00:29:41,912
Ben, I have to
get up on the stand

511
00:29:41,981 --> 00:29:44,080
and out loud, under oath,

512
00:29:44,149 --> 00:29:47,450
for the benefit of
everybody who voted for me

513
00:29:47,519 --> 00:29:50,820
and elected me, tell
them that I'm innocent.

514
00:29:50,889 --> 00:29:53,223
I'm against it.

515
00:29:57,363 --> 00:30:00,063
But okay.

516
00:30:03,202 --> 00:30:06,136
Senator Dolan,

517
00:30:06,205 --> 00:30:09,139
did you go to see Ron Winfield

518
00:30:09,208 --> 00:30:10,974
the night he was murdered?

519
00:30:11,043 --> 00:30:12,687
Yes, I did. Uh-huh.

520
00:30:12,711 --> 00:30:15,345
And, uh, when did
you go see him?

521
00:30:15,413 --> 00:30:17,425
I arrived at his house
at approximately 7:30.

522
00:30:17,449 --> 00:30:18,815
Mm-hmm.

523
00:30:18,884 --> 00:30:20,817
Why did you go see him?

524
00:30:20,886 --> 00:30:22,864
To see if he intended
to reveal the identity

525
00:30:22,888 --> 00:30:24,933
of the woman that I'd been
having a relationship with

526
00:30:24,957 --> 00:30:26,968
in the next installment
of his comic strip.

527
00:30:26,992 --> 00:30:28,970
And did he? Yes.

528
00:30:28,994 --> 00:30:31,127
Who was this woman?

529
00:30:31,196 --> 00:30:33,496
Sandra Hopkins.

530
00:30:33,565 --> 00:30:36,243
State Superintendent of Schools.

531
00:30:36,267 --> 00:30:37,278
Yes.

532
00:30:37,302 --> 00:30:38,702
We met last year.

533
00:30:38,770 --> 00:30:40,248
Saw each other on
business several times,

534
00:30:40,272 --> 00:30:42,038
and we fell in love.

535
00:30:42,107 --> 00:30:43,973
Well, what happened when, uh,

536
00:30:44,042 --> 00:30:46,643
Ronald Winfield told you

537
00:30:46,712 --> 00:30:49,345
he was gonna expose
your relationship with her?

538
00:30:49,414 --> 00:30:50,425
I got angry.

539
00:30:50,449 --> 00:30:52,382
He got angry.

540
00:30:52,451 --> 00:30:54,384
Push came to shove, literally.

541
00:30:54,453 --> 00:30:56,386
He hit me, I hit him.

542
00:30:56,455 --> 00:30:58,099
He fell backwards
against the fireplace.

543
00:30:58,123 --> 00:30:59,255
Mm-hmm. Did you hit him

544
00:30:59,324 --> 00:31:01,136
again after that?
No, sir, I did not.

545
00:31:01,160 --> 00:31:03,893
And in what condition was he

546
00:31:03,962 --> 00:31:05,328
when you left?

547
00:31:05,397 --> 00:31:07,408
He was alive...
Conscious and alive.

548
00:31:07,432 --> 00:31:08,865
In fact, he talked to me.

549
00:31:08,934 --> 00:31:11,079
When I took the next
installment of the comic strip

550
00:31:11,103 --> 00:31:12,235
he said go ahead,

551
00:31:12,304 --> 00:31:13,815
because he'd just
draw another one.

552
00:31:13,839 --> 00:31:15,438
What time was that?

553
00:31:15,507 --> 00:31:16,840
It was...

554
00:31:16,908 --> 00:31:18,408
7:40 when I left.

555
00:31:18,477 --> 00:31:19,954
No later. 7:40.

556
00:31:19,978 --> 00:31:23,413
Now...

557
00:31:23,482 --> 00:31:29,252
these photographs are
people's exhibit 23 through 25.

558
00:31:29,321 --> 00:31:31,922
And, uh, they were taken

559
00:31:31,990 --> 00:31:35,926
at approximately
9:40 p.m. at the scene.

560
00:31:35,994 --> 00:31:38,594
Uh, is this

561
00:31:38,663 --> 00:31:42,265
the way you remember
things when you left?

562
00:31:42,334 --> 00:31:43,933
No. In the first place,

563
00:31:44,002 --> 00:31:45,880
Winfield was lying on
the floor on the other side

564
00:31:45,904 --> 00:31:47,837
of the fireplace. Uh-huh.

565
00:31:47,906 --> 00:31:48,906
Anything else?

566
00:31:48,940 --> 00:31:50,674
Yes.

567
00:31:50,742 --> 00:31:53,755
The comic strip I took was
sitting on a stack of others

568
00:31:53,779 --> 00:31:55,712
right here on his desk.

569
00:31:55,781 --> 00:31:59,382
In both of these photos that...
Uh, those strips are gone.

570
00:31:59,451 --> 00:32:01,217
And you didn't take 'em?

571
00:32:01,286 --> 00:32:02,764
No, sir. I just took the one.

572
00:32:02,788 --> 00:32:04,721
Mm-hmm.

573
00:32:04,790 --> 00:32:07,724
Is there anything else,
uh, in these pictures

574
00:32:07,793 --> 00:32:10,426
that's, uh, different from
what you remember?

575
00:32:10,496 --> 00:32:11,672
Yes.

576
00:32:11,696 --> 00:32:15,565
Ron Winfield was alive
when I left the house.

577
00:32:15,633 --> 00:32:17,612
He was conscious.
He was talking.

578
00:32:17,636 --> 00:32:19,235
He was alive.

579
00:32:19,304 --> 00:32:21,237
I did not kill Ron Winfield.

580
00:32:21,306 --> 00:32:23,740
Intentionally or otherwise.

581
00:32:25,844 --> 00:32:28,144
Thank you, Senator.

582
00:32:28,213 --> 00:32:30,024
Your witness.

583
00:32:30,048 --> 00:32:34,150
Senator Dolan, why did you go
to Mr. Winfield's house that night?

584
00:32:34,219 --> 00:32:35,652
As I already said,

585
00:32:35,721 --> 00:32:37,565
to see if he intended
to reveal the identity

586
00:32:37,589 --> 00:32:38,855
of the woman I'd been seeing.

587
00:32:38,924 --> 00:32:40,902
Why didn't you just
call him on the phone?

588
00:32:40,926 --> 00:32:42,225
I did.

589
00:32:42,294 --> 00:32:45,061
His assistant said he
would return my phone call.

590
00:32:45,130 --> 00:32:46,429
He never did.

591
00:32:46,498 --> 00:32:50,400
Did, uh, you use profanity
when you spoke to his assistant?

592
00:32:50,469 --> 00:32:52,235
Yes, I did.

593
00:32:52,304 --> 00:32:53,569
Because you were angry?

594
00:32:53,638 --> 00:32:54,871
Yes.

595
00:32:54,940 --> 00:32:56,306
Why?

596
00:32:56,375 --> 00:32:58,386
Because he was going to

597
00:32:58,410 --> 00:33:01,411
expose my
relationship with Sandra.

598
00:33:01,479 --> 00:33:04,214
That would destroy our careers.

599
00:33:04,283 --> 00:33:08,051
Oh, so what you're saying is,

600
00:33:08,120 --> 00:33:10,553
you didn't go to
Winfield's house

601
00:33:10,622 --> 00:33:13,056
to see if he intended
to expose you,

602
00:33:13,125 --> 00:33:15,391
because you already knew
what he was going to do.

603
00:33:15,460 --> 00:33:17,193
You went there to
stop him, didn't you?

604
00:33:17,262 --> 00:33:18,473
Well, in-in a way, yes.

605
00:33:18,497 --> 00:33:20,463
You argued with him, didn't you?

606
00:33:20,532 --> 00:33:21,965
Yes.

607
00:33:22,034 --> 00:33:24,000
You hit him, didn't you? Yes...

608
00:33:24,069 --> 00:33:27,003
You hit him so hard his head
hit the fireplace, didn't you?

609
00:33:27,072 --> 00:33:29,149
Yes, but that was... You hit him
and you killed him, didn't you?

610
00:33:29,173 --> 00:33:31,107
No!

611
00:33:31,176 --> 00:33:34,444
His hitting his head
like that was an accident.

612
00:33:34,513 --> 00:33:36,846
Then why didn't you
call the paramedics?

613
00:33:36,915 --> 00:33:38,881
Because he was
alive when I left.

614
00:33:38,950 --> 00:33:41,551
You didn't call them
because he was dead.

615
00:33:41,620 --> 00:33:43,198
Just as you intended,
isn't that right,

616
00:33:43,222 --> 00:33:44,932
Senator? Object!

617
00:33:44,956 --> 00:33:47,356
This isn't a question, it's
an argument. Withdrawn.

618
00:33:47,659 --> 00:33:49,859
Nothing further.

619
00:34:05,477 --> 00:34:07,410
Otis!

620
00:34:09,515 --> 00:34:11,948
Get lucky?

621
00:34:12,017 --> 00:34:13,017
Uh, not yet.

622
00:34:13,052 --> 00:34:14,451
You know, those cans

623
00:34:14,520 --> 00:34:16,331
haven't been brought down
to the street since Ron died.

624
00:34:16,355 --> 00:34:18,475
Some of that stuff's
been in there for weeks.

625
00:34:18,824 --> 00:34:21,524
I know.

626
00:34:34,372 --> 00:34:36,183
That's one of the rough drafts

627
00:34:36,207 --> 00:34:38,341
of one of the missing strips.

628
00:34:38,409 --> 00:34:41,244
Did he ever mention anything
about Sunset Condominiums?

629
00:34:41,312 --> 00:34:42,456
Huh-uh.

630
00:34:42,480 --> 00:34:45,181
These trees are, uh...

631
00:34:45,250 --> 00:34:46,716
Birch?

632
00:34:46,784 --> 00:34:48,985
Uh, no, aspen.

633
00:34:51,323 --> 00:34:52,355
Aspen.

634
00:35:38,636 --> 00:35:40,904
Well...

635
00:35:40,973 --> 00:35:45,241
the Sunset Condominiums
are in Aspen.

636
00:35:45,310 --> 00:35:49,345
Senator Hopkins has been
paying taxes and utilities

637
00:35:49,414 --> 00:35:52,649
on a unit owned by
his six-year-old nephew.

638
00:35:52,718 --> 00:35:53,994
Six years old, huh?

639
00:35:54,018 --> 00:35:55,018
Yeah.

640
00:35:56,054 --> 00:35:57,720
What's the good
senator been up to?

641
00:35:57,789 --> 00:35:59,600
He had a two-hour
lunch at his club,

642
00:35:59,624 --> 00:36:01,101
and then he rushed over here.

643
00:36:01,125 --> 00:36:03,685
Didn't Jefferies
Construction just land

644
00:36:03,728 --> 00:36:05,795
some big highway jobs?

645
00:36:05,864 --> 00:36:07,998
A lot. All state-funded.

646
00:36:08,066 --> 00:36:10,211
Think there's a connection?

647
00:36:10,235 --> 00:36:14,271
Oh, yeah, there's a connection.

648
00:36:14,339 --> 00:36:18,341
And it's right there on the
seat of that boy's pants.

649
00:36:41,599 --> 00:36:44,334
Mr. Matlock, is that you?

650
00:36:44,402 --> 00:36:46,035
Yeah.

651
00:36:52,811 --> 00:36:54,355
Otis, get off him! Lie down!

652
00:37:01,386 --> 00:37:03,386
Look at your suit.

653
00:37:03,455 --> 00:37:04,821
It was my favorite.

654
00:37:04,890 --> 00:37:06,000
I'm sorry.

655
00:37:06,024 --> 00:37:07,735
I took him out to the park.

656
00:37:07,759 --> 00:37:09,303
The ground must have been wet.

657
00:37:09,327 --> 00:37:10,607
So, you getting anywhere?

658
00:37:10,662 --> 00:37:15,231
Yeah. I got a pretty
good idea of who did it.

659
00:37:15,300 --> 00:37:18,735
Still I got to prove it.

660
00:37:18,804 --> 00:37:20,202
That's a shame.

661
00:37:20,271 --> 00:37:24,307
It was my favorite and new...

662
00:37:37,489 --> 00:37:43,125
Well, good dog.

663
00:37:43,194 --> 00:37:44,727
Good dog!

664
00:38:18,396 --> 00:38:19,795
<i>Senator Hopkins,</i>

665
00:38:19,864 --> 00:38:22,131
you're chairman

666
00:38:22,200 --> 00:38:24,567
of the Georgia State Senate
Public Works Commitee,

667
00:38:24,636 --> 00:38:25,636
is that right?

668
00:38:25,671 --> 00:38:26,951
That's correct.

669
00:38:27,005 --> 00:38:28,571
Now what does your committee do?

670
00:38:28,639 --> 00:38:31,152
We see to it that the
great state of Georgia gets

671
00:38:31,176 --> 00:38:33,554
the highest-quality roads,
schools and office builidings

672
00:38:33,578 --> 00:38:36,379
at the lowest-possible
cost to the taxpayer.

673
00:38:36,448 --> 00:38:38,259
So you decide what
needs to be built,

674
00:38:38,283 --> 00:38:41,884
and then you solicit bids
from private contractors?

675
00:38:41,953 --> 00:38:43,719
That's correct.

676
00:38:43,788 --> 00:38:45,755
And the lowest bid gets the job?

677
00:38:45,824 --> 00:38:47,104
Not necessarily.

678
00:38:47,158 --> 00:38:49,592
But that usually is the
way it works, isn't it?

679
00:38:49,661 --> 00:38:52,428
Usually, I suppose, yes.

680
00:38:52,497 --> 00:38:53,696
Do you know

681
00:38:53,764 --> 00:38:56,532
about these bids
on ongoing projects?

682
00:38:56,601 --> 00:38:58,779
I have nothing to do
with the selection process.

683
00:38:58,803 --> 00:39:01,303
You could find out if you
wanted to, couldn't you?

684
00:39:01,372 --> 00:39:03,272
If I wanted to, yes, but, uh...

685
00:39:03,341 --> 00:39:04,341
Are you familiar

686
00:39:04,409 --> 00:39:07,443
with Jefferies
Construction Company?

687
00:39:07,511 --> 00:39:10,580
Yes, they've done
work for the state.

688
00:39:13,351 --> 00:39:17,520
Ten public-funded jobs
in the last two years.

689
00:39:17,589 --> 00:39:20,456
Bid on 12, got ten.

690
00:39:20,525 --> 00:39:23,192
I'd say that's
something of a record.

691
00:39:23,261 --> 00:39:24,561
Not only was their bid

692
00:39:24,629 --> 00:39:26,295
the lowest, it was the last.

693
00:39:26,364 --> 00:39:30,332
Almost as if someone were
keeping them informed on the bid

694
00:39:30,401 --> 00:39:32,902
so that they could be sure
and ease theirs in just under.

695
00:39:32,971 --> 00:39:34,704
That's impossible.

696
00:39:34,772 --> 00:39:35,772
Why?

697
00:39:35,807 --> 00:39:37,006
Because that's illegal.

698
00:39:37,074 --> 00:39:38,408
Yeah, but, but, someone could

699
00:39:38,477 --> 00:39:43,279
keep the president of
Jefferies Construction informed

700
00:39:43,347 --> 00:39:45,226
on the bid, so that
he could be certain

701
00:39:45,250 --> 00:39:47,851
that his company's was
the lowest, couldn't he?

702
00:39:47,919 --> 00:39:48,851
How about you, Senator?

703
00:39:48,919 --> 00:39:50,198
Absolutely not. Objection!

704
00:39:50,222 --> 00:39:51,498
This is completely
irresponsible.

705
00:39:51,522 --> 00:39:53,857
Nothing in Mr. Matlock's
question has anything

706
00:39:53,925 --> 00:39:55,002
to do with the evidence!

707
00:39:55,026 --> 00:39:56,492
I tell you what, Your Honor,

708
00:39:56,561 --> 00:39:59,262
if you'll allow me to
continue, I'll prove it.

709
00:39:59,331 --> 00:40:00,763
Overruled for now.

710
00:40:00,832 --> 00:40:08,037
In the last six
weeks of his life,

711
00:40:08,105 --> 00:40:13,275
Ron Winfield planted
the word "sunset"

712
00:40:13,344 --> 00:40:15,445
ten times in his comic strip.

713
00:40:15,513 --> 00:40:19,114
See on the billboard there
and on the bus stop bench

714
00:40:19,183 --> 00:40:22,385
and on the book? Why
would he do that, huh?

715
00:40:22,454 --> 00:40:23,686
How about this?

716
00:40:23,754 --> 00:40:25,587
He wanted to remind
one of his readers

717
00:40:25,656 --> 00:40:28,658
that he knew something
about that reader

718
00:40:28,726 --> 00:40:31,227
that he could make
public at any time.

719
00:40:31,296 --> 00:40:33,095
It was kind of a code.

720
00:40:33,164 --> 00:40:34,908
A secret message,
wasn't it, Senator?

721
00:40:34,932 --> 00:40:37,199
I don't know what on
Earth you're talking about.

722
00:40:37,268 --> 00:40:39,935
Last year, the president
of Jefferies Construction

723
00:40:40,004 --> 00:40:45,274
bought a condominium
at Aspen, Colorado,

724
00:40:45,343 --> 00:40:46,509
for a half-million dollars.

725
00:40:46,578 --> 00:40:48,455
But he didn't
buy it for himself.

726
00:40:48,479 --> 00:40:50,157
He signed it over
to someone else.

727
00:40:50,181 --> 00:40:53,148
He signed it over
to a person named

728
00:40:53,217 --> 00:40:56,452
Joel Martin, your
six-year-old nephew.

729
00:40:56,521 --> 00:40:58,332
I don't know
anything about this.

730
00:40:58,356 --> 00:41:00,134
Are you saying that
wasn't a kickback?

731
00:41:00,158 --> 00:41:01,457
Of course it wasn't.

732
00:41:01,526 --> 00:41:03,059
That it was just a coincidence?

733
00:41:03,127 --> 00:41:04,594
It must be.

734
00:41:04,663 --> 00:41:06,541
That the president of
Jefferies Construction wasn't

735
00:41:06,565 --> 00:41:09,532
paying you back for all
those big fat jobs you got them

736
00:41:09,601 --> 00:41:12,835
with a big fat
chunk of real estate?

737
00:41:12,904 --> 00:41:15,037
Absolutely not.

738
00:41:15,106 --> 00:41:17,906
You're saying that it's a
coincidence that the name

739
00:41:17,975 --> 00:41:22,044
of the condominium
project at Aspen is Sunset?

740
00:41:22,113 --> 00:41:24,514
All I know is I've
done nothing wrong,

741
00:41:24,582 --> 00:41:26,260
and I bitterly resent
these allegations.

742
00:41:26,284 --> 00:41:30,619
Ron Winfield found out
about your arrangement

743
00:41:30,688 --> 00:41:32,054
with Jefferies Construction.

744
00:41:32,123 --> 00:41:33,501
There is no arrangment.

745
00:41:33,525 --> 00:41:36,325
He was blackmailing
you, wasn't he?

746
00:41:36,394 --> 00:41:37,326
No.

747
00:41:37,395 --> 00:41:39,195
Only he didn't want cash,

748
00:41:39,264 --> 00:41:40,629
he wanted dirt...

749
00:41:40,698 --> 00:41:44,366
Inside information on political
shenanigans he could use

750
00:41:44,435 --> 00:41:45,902
in his comic strip.

751
00:41:45,970 --> 00:41:50,306
And you knew if you didn't
come through, he'd expose you.

752
00:41:50,374 --> 00:41:53,476
And that would not only
mean the end of your job,

753
00:41:53,545 --> 00:41:54,610
you'd go to jail.

754
00:41:54,679 --> 00:41:56,712
So you gave him what he wanted.

755
00:41:56,781 --> 00:41:58,692
Is that how he found
out about your wife's affair

756
00:41:58,716 --> 00:41:59,716
with Senator Dolan?

757
00:41:59,750 --> 00:42:02,351
Are you out of your mind?

758
00:42:02,420 --> 00:42:03,519
Over and over again,

759
00:42:03,588 --> 00:42:06,188
he used the word
"sunset" in his comic strip.

760
00:42:06,257 --> 00:42:09,292
It must have driven you
crazy trying to figure out

761
00:42:09,360 --> 00:42:11,160
what he'd do next.

762
00:42:11,228 --> 00:42:13,929
And so finally, that night

763
00:42:13,998 --> 00:42:16,965
you went over to
his house, didn't you?

764
00:42:17,034 --> 00:42:18,178
I did no such thing.

765
00:42:18,202 --> 00:42:19,813
I don't know what
you had in mind

766
00:42:19,837 --> 00:42:21,604
when you went into that room,

767
00:42:21,673 --> 00:42:25,007
but I'm pretty sure that when
you saw that he was hurt,

768
00:42:25,076 --> 00:42:29,044
and he told you that
Senator Dolan had done it,

769
00:42:29,113 --> 00:42:32,414
you hit his head in that
fireplace a second time

770
00:42:32,483 --> 00:42:33,549
and killed him.

771
00:42:33,618 --> 00:42:35,618
And then you took
the comic strips

772
00:42:35,687 --> 00:42:38,687
so that no one would
be able to connect you

773
00:42:38,756 --> 00:42:39,933
with the condominium at Aspen.

774
00:42:39,957 --> 00:42:41,123
That's a lie!

775
00:42:41,192 --> 00:42:44,760
And knowing that Ron
Winfield kept solid evidence

776
00:42:44,829 --> 00:42:47,296
to back the allegations
in his comic strip,

777
00:42:47,365 --> 00:42:49,410
you sent someone from
Jefferies Construction

778
00:42:49,434 --> 00:42:52,068
to ransack his house,
steal the evidence,

779
00:42:52,137 --> 00:42:53,369
and destroy it, didn't you?

780
00:42:53,437 --> 00:42:56,505
Everything you just
said is an out-and-out lie!

781
00:42:56,574 --> 00:42:58,574
You didn't go to
his house that night?

782
00:42:58,643 --> 00:43:01,477
I've never been to Ron
Winfield's house in my life.

783
00:43:01,546 --> 00:43:03,586
Never? Never.

784
00:43:05,116 --> 00:43:08,718
Would you tell the court
whose suit jacket this is?

785
00:43:08,786 --> 00:43:13,622
It looks like mine.

786
00:43:13,691 --> 00:43:14,735
It is mine.

787
00:43:14,759 --> 00:43:15,970
Where did you get it?

788
00:43:15,994 --> 00:43:18,628
The police with a warrant
took it from your closet

789
00:43:18,696 --> 00:43:20,329
this morning after you left.

790
00:43:20,398 --> 00:43:22,732
Is this the suit you wore
to his house that night?

791
00:43:22,801 --> 00:43:26,135
I just told you I have never
been to Ron Winfield's house.

792
00:43:26,204 --> 00:43:28,604
Why'd you take it to the
cleaners the next morning?

793
00:43:28,673 --> 00:43:29,716
I don't remember.

794
00:43:29,740 --> 00:43:31,507
Well, right here's the receipt.

795
00:43:31,576 --> 00:43:35,344
See? "8:45 A.M., April seventh."

796
00:43:35,413 --> 00:43:37,379
That's the day after the murder.

797
00:43:37,449 --> 00:43:41,484
It says, "Remove
stain on jacket front."

798
00:43:41,553 --> 00:43:46,756
Ron Winfield had a dog,
big friendly dog named "Otis."

799
00:43:46,825 --> 00:43:50,192
Liked to jump up on everybody
who came into the room

800
00:43:50,261 --> 00:43:51,994
and get dirt on your jacket.

801
00:43:52,063 --> 00:43:55,098
Have to take it to the cleaners.

802
00:43:55,166 --> 00:43:59,768
During the fight with
Mr. Winfield and Senator Dolan,

803
00:43:59,837 --> 00:44:02,505
a bottle of ink got
knocked off on the floor.

804
00:44:02,574 --> 00:44:04,773
See the ink there?

805
00:44:04,842 --> 00:44:06,809
The dog must have
walked through it.

806
00:44:06,878 --> 00:44:07,810
See his pawprints?

807
00:44:07,879 --> 00:44:09,823
What if that dog
walked through that ink

808
00:44:09,847 --> 00:44:14,083
after Senator Dolan
left and jumped up on

809
00:44:14,152 --> 00:44:15,863
the next person to
come into the room...

810
00:44:15,887 --> 00:44:17,787
The real killer?

811
00:44:17,855 --> 00:44:21,123
Why, he'd get, he'd
get ink on his suit front.

812
00:44:21,192 --> 00:44:22,892
Have to go to the cleaners.

813
00:44:22,961 --> 00:44:25,328
I spilled coffee on my suit.

814
00:44:25,396 --> 00:44:26,396
Maybe so.

815
00:44:26,464 --> 00:44:29,231
The cleaner was unable to say.

816
00:44:29,300 --> 00:44:32,067
It wasn't ink, Mr. Matlock.

817
00:44:32,136 --> 00:44:34,370
Now for the last time, I
was not at that house.

818
00:44:34,438 --> 00:44:35,678
That night or any other night.

819
00:44:35,740 --> 00:44:36,839
How about this?

820
00:44:36,908 --> 00:44:39,308
This is a piece of Ron
Winfield's stationery.

821
00:44:39,377 --> 00:44:43,279
Officer Danny Levine's
prepared to testify that he found it

822
00:44:43,347 --> 00:44:45,448
in the pocket of this suit

823
00:44:45,517 --> 00:44:47,784
this morning when he
took it from your home.

824
00:44:47,852 --> 00:44:50,620
And a handwriting
expert is prepared to testify

825
00:44:50,688 --> 00:44:52,789
that this is Ron
Winfield's handwriting.

826
00:44:52,857 --> 00:44:54,424
This is a list of calls

827
00:44:54,492 --> 00:44:57,760
he received the day
he was murdered.

828
00:44:57,828 --> 00:44:58,861
You took this

829
00:44:58,929 --> 00:45:01,664
because you didn't
want anyone to know

830
00:45:01,733 --> 00:45:03,833
that you were so
anxious to talk to him

831
00:45:03,901 --> 00:45:06,301
that you called him
five times that day.

832
00:45:06,371 --> 00:45:08,738
You took this and
the comic strips

833
00:45:08,806 --> 00:45:14,043
after you killed
him, didn't you?

834
00:45:31,429 --> 00:45:33,963
We find the
defendant not guilty.

835
00:45:34,032 --> 00:45:36,065
Having found the
defendant not guilty,

836
00:45:36,133 --> 00:45:37,599
this court is adjourned.

837
00:45:38,836 --> 00:45:41,770
Thank you, both
of you, very much.

838
00:45:41,839 --> 00:45:43,706
Now you can get
on with your life.

839
00:45:43,774 --> 00:45:44,885
What's left of it.

840
00:45:44,909 --> 00:45:46,620
I still have a feeling
I'm gonna have

841
00:45:46,644 --> 00:45:48,556
to step down even with
the not-guilty verdict.

842
00:45:48,580 --> 00:45:50,213
There are a lot of
reporters out here.

843
00:45:50,281 --> 00:45:51,992
Want me to run
interference for you?

844
00:45:52,016 --> 00:45:52,948
No, that's all right.

845
00:45:53,017 --> 00:45:54,483
I got to face the
music sometime.

846
00:45:54,552 --> 00:45:55,818
It might as well be now.

847
00:45:55,886 --> 00:45:57,986
Thank you again. Bye.

848
00:45:59,056 --> 00:46:00,723
I'll go with you.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
